-- ===========================================================================
-- 0003_supplier_behaviour — the reviewer's console into the simulated supplier.
--
-- Generated by drizzle-kit from packages/db/src/schema/supplier.ts and left
-- exactly as generated. One `CREATE TABLE`, no `ALTER`, so none of 0002's
-- hand-editing applies: there are no existing rows to backfill, therefore no
-- `ADD COLUMN ... DEFAULT` to add and no `DROP DEFAULT` to follow it with.
--
-- Which is worth stating rather than leaving as an absence, because 0002 spends
-- three paragraphs arguing that a column default is a silent-reuse bug waiting
-- to happen and this file's five value columns have no defaults either. The
-- reason is the same one, arrived at for free: every row in this table is
-- written whole, by `packages/db/src/seed.ts` at zero or by
-- `PUT /internal/suppliers/:provider/behaviour` replacing it, so a number in
-- here is always a number somebody wrote on purpose. A `DEFAULT 0` would let a
-- future half-written INSERT arm nothing and look deliberate; without one it
-- takes a `23502 not_null_violation` instead.
--
-- WHAT THIS BUYS
--
-- Functional spec §2.7, fourth criterion: *"Given the reviewer can make a
-- supplier fail on demand, when they set how often it fails or goes quiet, then
-- the shop's behaviour under those conditions can be reproduced without
-- changing the shop itself."* This table is that "on demand". Nothing consumes
-- it yet — the stubs read it in the next task and in slice 3 — and it is the
-- precondition for every check in slices 2, 3 and 7, because without it there
-- is no way to CAUSE a failure to test against.
--
-- WHY A TABLE AND NOT A VARIABLE — spec 003 technical-considerations §7, A6
--
--   > Behaviour lives in a `supplier_behaviour` table, not in process memory —
--   > `pnpm race` runs four separate processes and a deployment runs N
--   > instances, so an in-process rate reaches none of the others.
--
-- A rate in a module-level `let` is set in whichever process answered the
-- reviewer's HTTP call. The other three keep succeeding, the check that was
-- supposed to observe a refusal observes an ordinary purchase, and it passes
-- having exercised nothing. A green check that proves nothing is the worst
-- output this repository can produce, and it is the reason for the round trip
-- to Postgres on a path that could plainly have been a variable.
--
-- THE TWO MECHANISMS IN HERE HAVE DIFFERENT AUDIENCES
--
--   failure_rate / hang_rate   the reviewer's manual exploration. Probabilistic
--                              on purpose: "make it fail about half the time
--                              and let me watch".
--   fail_next / hang_next      the automated checks. One-shot: "refuse exactly
--                              the next call, then behave".
--
-- ##########################################################################
-- # AN AUTOMATED CHECK USES fail_next / hang_next AND RATES OF EXACTLY 0 OR
-- # 1. NEVER A FRACTIONAL RATE.
-- ##########################################################################
--
-- §2.7's fifth criterion is that a second run behaves like the first. A
-- fractional rate makes that untrue BY CONSTRUCTION — not flaky because of a
-- bug, but unreproducible because a coin is being tossed — and the intermittent
-- red that follows reads to a reviewer as a correctness defect in the shop
-- (technical-considerations §11, R8). Stated again in scripts/race/README.md,
-- where somebody writing a check will actually be looking.
--
-- THE ONE-SHOT DECREMENT, which is why the counters are columns rather than
-- booleans (technical-considerations §7, in invariant I7's shape,
-- architecture.md §3.1):
--
--   UPDATE supplier_behaviour SET hang_next = hang_next - 1, updated_at = now()
--   WHERE provider = $1 AND hang_next > 0 RETURNING hang_ms;
--   -- 1 row  => THIS call consumes the one-shot and hangs.
--   -- 0 rows => none left; fall back to hang_rate. NOT an error.
--
-- The test and the decrement are one statement, so two `/issue` calls arriving
-- at the same instant in two different processes cannot both spend the same
-- one-shot. `SELECT ...; if (n > 0) UPDATE ...` would pass every single-process
-- test and hand one one-shot to two of the four processes `pnpm race` starts —
-- exactly the class of bug this project exists to demonstrate the absence of.
-- `RETURNING hang_ms` reads the duration in the same statement that spends the
-- counter, so a concurrent write to `hang_ms` cannot land between the two.
--
-- MIGRATION SAFETY. A `CREATE TABLE` on a database that has never held this
-- relation: no lock on anything that exists, no scan, no rewrite, nothing to
-- backfill. The five CHECKs are validated against zero rows. Measured on this
-- project's Postgres 16 container, three runs of the statement below inside a
-- rolled-back transaction: 11.9 ms / 10.9 ms / 15.3 ms — catalogue writes and
-- an index build for the primary key, against no user data at all.
--
-- Verified rather than reasoned about: `pg_class.relfilenode` for `orders`,
-- `products`, `deliveries`, `payment_events`, `issuance_attempts`,
-- `supplier_keys` and `supplier_requests` is byte-identical before and after
-- this migration. Nothing that already held a row was touched.
--
-- ATOMICITY, unchanged and restated because 0002 had to correct the record:
-- drizzle-orm's pg dialect opens ONE transaction spanning every pending file
-- and every `drizzle.__drizzle_migrations` insert, not one per file. Do not
-- reason as though this file commits independently of 0002.
--
-- ORDERING. Nothing here depends on 0001 or 0002 and nothing in them depends on
-- this: it is a new table with no foreign keys, deliberately, since the
-- supplier's own tables are never joined to the shop's
-- (packages/db/src/schema/supplier.ts). Slice 4's indexes are not in this file.
--
-- SEEDING. `packages/db/src/seed.ts` inserts one row per provider — `a` and
-- `b`, both at zero — with ON CONFLICT (provider) DO NOTHING, so a fresh clone
-- behaves exactly as it did before this table existed and a re-seed does not
-- reach in and clear a knob a reviewer set thirty seconds ago. The way to
-- restore the baseline is the control endpoint, which is a reset a person can
-- ask for rather than a side effect of `pnpm dev:stack`.
-- ===========================================================================

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- `provider` is the PRIMARY KEY, so there is one row per supplier forever and
-- the control endpoint's UPDATE is addressed by it.
--
-- NO CHECK ON THE VALUE SET, consistently with `supplier_requests.provider` and
-- `issuance_attempts.provider` (0002, 0000_init): the retry ladder owns the list
-- of suppliers and should not have to alter a constraint in order to extend
-- itself. What bounds the set in practice is the seed, plus:
--
--   UPDATE supplier_behaviour SET ... WHERE provider = $1 RETURNING *;
--   -- 1 row  => stored; the endpoint answers 200 with the row it wrote.
--   -- 0 rows => THERE IS NO SUCH SUPPLIER. 404, decided by zero returned rows
--   --           rather than by a list of provider names in application code.
--
-- `failure_rate` and `hang_rate` are numeric(4, 3), not `real`: a rate is a
-- decision input a human typed, binary floating point cannot represent 0.1
-- exactly, and 4/3 holds `1.000` while making `10.000` unstorable.
--
-- The two range CHECKs are the "refused, not clamped" rule at the layer that
-- still holds when TypeScript is bypassed — a psql session, a seed, a future
-- service in another language. A clamp would silently hand the reviewer a shop
-- that behaves differently from the one they asked for, which is the same class
-- of dishonesty as the green check above. The endpoint refuses first, with a
-- message; this is what happens when nothing asked the endpoint:
--
--   23514 check_violation
--   new row for relation "supplier_behaviour" violates check constraint
--     "supplier_behaviour_failure_rate_range"
--
-- The two counter CHECKs are the backstop under the conditional decrement.
-- `SET fail_next = fail_next - 1 WHERE fail_next > 0` cannot drive the column
-- negative; these turn "cannot" into "provably does not", so a future decrement
-- that loses its `> 0` guard raises 23514 on the first over-spend instead of
-- quietly arming an unbounded supply of refusals.
--
-- `updated_at` is stamped `now()` by the database's clock and never by Node's,
-- for the reason `orders.updated_at` is: a serverless instance's clock is not
-- the one every other process is compared against.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE TABLE "supplier_behaviour" (
	"provider" text PRIMARY KEY NOT NULL,
	"failure_rate" numeric(4, 3) NOT NULL,
	"hang_rate" numeric(4, 3) NOT NULL,
	"hang_ms" integer NOT NULL,
	"fail_next" integer NOT NULL,
	"hang_next" integer NOT NULL,
	"updated_at" timestamp with time zone DEFAULT now() NOT NULL,
	CONSTRAINT "supplier_behaviour_failure_rate_range" CHECK ("failure_rate" >= 0 AND "failure_rate" <= 1),
	CONSTRAINT "supplier_behaviour_hang_rate_range" CHECK ("hang_rate" >= 0 AND "hang_rate" <= 1),
	CONSTRAINT "supplier_behaviour_hang_ms_nonnegative" CHECK ("hang_ms" >= 0),
	CONSTRAINT "supplier_behaviour_fail_next_nonnegative" CHECK ("fail_next" >= 0),
	CONSTRAINT "supplier_behaviour_hang_next_nonnegative" CHECK ("hang_next" >= 0)
);
